Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models are harder to serve than dense models of comparable quality because they trade compute efficiency for memory and communication overhead. A dense model activates all its parameters for every token, making inference predictable and cache-friendly. An MoE model, by contrast, routes each token to only a few experts out of many, so the full parameter set must still reside in memory even though only a fraction is used per token. This creates a large memory footprint relative to active compute, forcing expensive multi-GPU deployments. Routing also introduces dynamic, data-dependent computation: different tokens hit different experts, causing load imbalance, synchronization stalls, and all-to-all communication across devices. Batching becomes harder because experts receive variable token counts. Consequently, MoE serving demands careful parallelism, expert placement, and load balancing, making it significantly more complex and less efficient to operate at scale than a dense model of similar quality.
